WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.360
Asus.
The dominant motherboard producer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:10.880
trusted by gamers for being premium, and reliable.
But, they were wronging customers for years.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.880
This is how Asus was screwing&nbsp;
consumers, and how one YouTuber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:25.520
caught them red-handed, not once, but twice.
And even confronted them face to face.&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:35.520
Asus was at the top of the game.
Out of 37 million projected motherboard sales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.640
15 million were from Asus, with&nbsp;
Gigabyte far behind at 9 million.&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:46.880
Asus even held over 38% of the global&nbsp;
market share, and was a $10 billion company.&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:50.160
And perhaps even stronger, was their reputation.&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:56.080
They were the enthusiast's choice. They were&nbsp;
innovative, reliable, and had great performance.&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.080
Their gaming brand: Republic&nbsp;
of Gamers, is also huge.&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:06.000
And Asus isn't just motherboards either.
They compete across the full stack: GPUs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.840
laptops, monitors, gaming handhelds, pretty&nbsp;
much every product line in PC gaming.&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.640
But, soon, cracks in Asus's&nbsp;
pristine brand began to show.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:23.600
In early 2023, users were noticing AMD&nbsp;
Ryzen CPUs, specifically the 7000X3D line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:29.600
were physically bulging and charred.
Users were, understandably, very alarmed.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.840
When investigated, the community&nbsp;
found a common factor: Asus.&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:40.560
Specifically, the ASUS 7800x3d Motherboard.
Customers began to compile more cases of this.&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.440
What was going on?
Well, that question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.160
brings us to Asus's worst nightmare.
In the comments of one of the posts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:52.800
a user asked if they could buy both&nbsp;
the board, and the CPU, to investigate.&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.520
This was GamersNexus, a hardware&nbsp;
review and tech journalism channel.&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:17.760
And soon they revealed their findings.
Here's what happened.&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.200
In the ASUS BIOS, or "basic input/output system",&nbsp;
there's an overclock setting called EXPO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:28.880
which allows RAM to run at its high advertised&nbsp;
speeds, instead of the default, slower speed.&nbsp;

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:32.880
Gamers often overclock their components&nbsp;
to get the most performance out of them.&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.600
When you overclock with a setting like EXPO, the&nbsp;
motherboard also needs to increase a separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:45.600
voltage, called SoC, which runs through the CPU.
But... ASUS was setting this way, way too high.&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:51.920
The BIOS on the ASUS motherboard showed&nbsp;
1.35 volts, already high for an AMD CPU.&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.000
But physically measuring the motherboard, Steve&nbsp;
from GamersNexus showed the real number was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.040
between 1.39 or even 1.4 volts.
Doesn't sound like much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:26.720
but it was enough to physically burn the CPU.
Yet, Asus, somehow in attempting to fix it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:32.880
was about to make the problem much worse.
Asus sent out BIOS version 1410.&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.640
And users noticed something very strange.
"ASUS does not give any warranties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:46.160
as to the suitability, compatibility, or usability&nbsp;
of the UEFI, its firmware or any of its content".&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:51.120
That's right, while rolling out a fix, they&nbsp;
quietly added that downloading it would void&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.160
your motherboard warranty.
Insane.&nbsp;

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.880
But, it somehow gets worse.
Asus noted that "any intentional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.440
manipulation can damage the processor, socket&nbsp;
and motherboard". (Show quote at 23:42).&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:10.560
They're right, but here they're referring to EXPO.
There's intentional manipulation, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.680
there's running EXPO, a heavily&nbsp;
marketed, widely used feature.&nbsp;

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:20.640
ASUS also quietly removed the older&nbsp;
BIOS versions from their download page.&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:26.720
So, customers could stay on the original BIOS&nbsp;
and risk burning their CPU, or install the fix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:33.120
and lose their warranty. Great.
Either way, ASUS wins. At least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.120 --> 00:05:01.440
that's probably what they thought.
Steve also pointed out that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:07.600
if you did follow Asus's guidance to not&nbsp;
use EXPO, you'd see major performance drops.&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.560
Y'know, on the big fancy gaming motherboard&nbsp;
you spent hundreds of dollars on.&nbsp;

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:18.960
This follow-up video passed 1.1 million views&nbsp;
in just one month, and soon, it began to spread.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:38.560
"For those who dare" went from a live love laugh&nbsp;
marketing tag to an actual threat. Awesome"&nbsp;

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:43.360
"I don't understand how Asus went from "slightly&nbsp;
more expensive but feature rich and reliable" a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:50.240
decade ago to "overpriced gimmicks and missing out&nbsp;
on important features". Massive shame. Asus used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:54.560
to be one of the brands I could trust."
It was all falling apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.560 --> 00:06:00.400
including Asus's prestigious reputation.
The thing is, even with the burning socket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:13.680
things could have been very different.
So, what happened?&nbsp;

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:17.200
Asus released a statement:
"We want to address the concerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:21.040
that have been raised by our users about&nbsp;
whether recent BIOS updates will impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:26.720
the warranty of ASUS AM5 motherboards."
They outlined that, contrary to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:32.080
BIOS update, the warranty would cover&nbsp;
the motherboard, including EXPO use.&nbsp;

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:34.640
Though, it doesn't seem&nbsp;
like there was compensation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:42.000
for those who already had burned CPUs.
And unfortunately, this was just the beginning.&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.160
This somehow, wasn't even the&nbsp;
worst thing Asus would do.&nbsp;

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:52.640
And, guess who was about to catch them red-handed?
But this time it would be different, and would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.640 --> 00:07:02.240
set off a long string of dominoes, ending&nbsp;
with Steve confronting Asus face to face.

