00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:23.200
But unbeknownst to ASUS,&nbsp;
this would be an experiment.&nbsp;

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:45.840
They documented everything.
As soon as Asus received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.840 --> 00:11:18.000
the ROG Ally, the red flags appeared.
Asus claimed the device had "signs of damage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:23.200
which is not covered under warranty".
So, what was the damage?&nbsp;

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:38.080
But, it gets even weirder.
Asus didn't even acknowledge the real problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:43.600
the joystick issue, which was under warranty.
And Asus said that, if payment wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:48.720
received, it would be sent back…&nbsp;
unrepaired and even disassembled.&nbsp;

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:53.440
You would send Asus a faulty product,&nbsp;
they would ignore it, void your warranty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:58.480
charge you for something else, then threaten&nbsp;
to send you back a truly unusable product.

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:24.240
But what made all this&nbsp;
worse, was the time pressure.

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:57.200
Asus, eventually, after a lot of persuasion&nbsp;
