00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:11.280
On May 16, 2024, five days after the video,&nbsp;
Asus issued a statement… a weird one.&nbsp;

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:16.480
"Currently, we perform a full analysis of&nbsp;
devices sent for RMA, and send customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:22.560
a comprehensive list of available repairs,&nbsp;
free and paid, in our messaging to customers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:27.440
We understand this may have caused confusion&nbsp;
when a customer has only ordered a specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:33.840
repair. We will no longer automatically offer&nbsp;
repair quotations for cosmetic imperfections"&nbsp;

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:38.560
"We deeply apologize to our customers and the&nbsp;
community for any confusion and frustration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:44.320
they might have experienced from this."
Apparently, it was the customers who were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:49.200
"confused". And Asus was simply, and&nbsp;
graciously, giving consumers their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:54.240
options — when overcharging for non-existent&nbsp;
damage, voiding your warranty, and threatening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.240 --> 00:15:00.080
to send back your device unassembled.
People weren't happy, understandably so.&nbsp;

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:06.480
The backlash began to escalate.
Soon, on May 23rd they released another statement.&nbsp;

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:11.520
A more comprehensive overhaul to repairs.
Promises of better communication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:16.640
documentation, changes to repair prices&nbsp;
— all of which were notorious before.&nbsp;

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:22.560
But, customers still weren't convinced.
"This is just a PR stunt to make it look like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:29.120
they care, and that they're doing something.&nbsp;
They won't change, this is who they are"&nbsp;

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:33.280
"From now on Asus goes on&nbsp;
my warranty scammer list".&nbsp;

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:38.080
Was anything going to change… really?
Two days later, GamersNexus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.080 --> 00:15:42.480
released a "Warranty Response Kit".
If real change was going to happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:46.320
customers had to make themselves heard.
But not to ASUS.&nbsp;

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:50.480
To the Federal Trade Commission.
The page detailed steps to file a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:53.570
complaint, along with everything a customer&nbsp;
would need to know. (show Warranty Fraud:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:56.000
How to File a Report section).
And that's not all.&nbsp;

00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:00.720
"We have begun the process of connecting with&nbsp;
Congressional and Senatorial representatives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:05.200
are contacting the Federal Trade Commission,&nbsp;
and have begun interviews with experts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:12.480
advocates for consumers on these matters."
This was a big problem for ASUS, because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:17.680
had already been on the FTC's watch list.
They were one of six companies to receive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:23.760
a warning letter about illegal warranty terms.
In the meantime however, GamersNexus was going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:29.440
to confront Asus, face to face, in Taiwan.
In their statements, their promises of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:34.960
"changes" were just broad statements.
What were they actually going to fix?

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:40.880
Across two different discussions over three hours,&nbsp;
Steve pressed ASUS on answers and commitments to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:46.320
improving RMA, to make them accountable.
And while there were some breakthroughs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:50.480
the results were mixed.
At the very least, Steve twisted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:57.040
their arm into agreeing to a list of changes.
Then, a month later, in July 2024, the FTC sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:02.640
a new round of warning letters to companies about&nbsp;
making warranties void, including ASRock, Zotac,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:10.080
and Gigabyte, though not ASUS — although they had&nbsp;
already received a warning from the FTC in 2018.&nbsp;

00:17:10.080 --> 00:17:15.600
And that brings us to ASUS today.
Has anything changed?&nbsp;

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:21.040
There are signs of improvement. Asus opened a&nbsp;
new email where customers with past disputed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:27.040
warranty claims could submit them for re-review.
And, Asus, to their credit, went back and settled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:32.960
over 120 cases, and reached out to over 300&nbsp;
customers who were incorrectly overcharged.&nbsp;

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:36.960
A new repair center also opened in&nbsp;
North America, instead of passing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.960 --> 00:17:42.880
repairs to third parties at a lower cost.
There's also more clarity, and any "customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:48.240
induced damage" cases have much more scrutiny.
They've simplified the RMA search system and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:51.920
removed shipping fees for mixed repairs.
A lot of good stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:58.560
to Asus's credit. But it's not perfect.
It seems like a few things are slipping through.&nbsp;

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:05.440
In December 2025, a customer sent a ROG&nbsp;
RTX 5090 to RMA after random black screens.&nbsp;

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:11.360
Asus declined the warranty, due to a crack so&nbsp;
small it was only visible under a microscope.&nbsp;

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:16.880
They quoted $4,661 CAD to replace&nbsp;
the card, even though brand new it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:22.640
usually retails around $4,059 CAD.
Even after months of back and forth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:29.840
the best they could do was a 50% discount — even&nbsp;
though it should be under warranty, at no cost.&nbsp;

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:35.200
Customers are still nervous about&nbsp;
buying ASUS, and I don't blame them.&nbsp;

00:18:35.200 --> 00:18:39.760
The damage has been done. Asus&nbsp;
betrayed customers' trust.&nbsp;

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:43.440
They tried to cut costs and void&nbsp;
warranties wherever possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:48.960
and their reputation has taken a huge hit.
They're still making money, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:55.680
but dedicated fans and enthusiasts have&nbsp;
moved on, and many will never return.&nbsp;

00:18:55.680 --> 00:19:00.240
It's going to take a long time&nbsp;
to rebuild that trust, if ever.

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:03.040
The crazy thing is, this isn't even the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:07.040
Steve from GamersNexus has gone&nbsp;
head to head with a tech giant.&nbsp;

